    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1494">

                <pb facs="1296" n="1038" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg1038"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 855.48/260</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gerard</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the</hi>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Secretary of State</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-05-04">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">May 4, 1915</hi>.</date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received May 6, 8 a. m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2156. Following received, from American Legation, Hague, and transmitted Foreign
                    Office April 21:</p>
                <pb facs="1297" n="1039" xml:id="pg1039"/>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>55, 20th. Commission requests you to ask German Government for safe-conduct
                        passes from Falmouth to Rotterdam for steamers <hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Ethelbrytha, Ardandiorg, carisbrooke, Yeddo, Horsa</hi>, carrying
                        cargoes which they have purchased <hi rendition="#italic">en route</hi>.
                        They propose that American Ambassador, London, be authorized to deliver the
                        passes on telegraphic advice for each steamer from German Legation, Hague.
                        They also request the same procedure in future for similar cases. We
                        understand German Legation here telegraphing their Government their
                        agreement to above. Matter is urgent on account of supplies for northern
                        France and to save demurrage on steamers.</p>
                </quote>
                <p>Foreign Office replies, dated 1st, received to-day:</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>German Government quite willing issue safe-conduct permits those ships
                        carrying cargoes bought afloat by commission for each passage from Falmouth
                        to Rotterdam provided vessels carry exclusively cargoes for relief
                        commission and are provided with markings commission clearly distinguishable
                        day and night. These permits would be issued by Imperial Legation, Hague,
                        pursuant instructions from Berlin and would be delivered to Rotterdam
                        representative relief commission for further transmission.</p>
                </quote>
                <p>Legation Hague informed direct.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gerard</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
